// =====================================================================
// AP06 — Entra Connect / Seamless SSO (AZUREADSSOACC$): on-prem -> cloud bridge
//        Chain: the attacker starts from the credential of the Entra Connect
//        SYNCHRONIZATION account (AZRANGE\Sync_AZRANGE) seeded with DCSync
//        rights  ->  performs DCSync (4662 + replication GUID) to extract the
//        Kerberos keys of the machine account AZUREADSSOACC$  ->  FORGES a silver
//        ticket for the SPN HTTP/autologon.microsoftazuread-sso.com impersonating ANY
//        synchronized user  ->  authenticates to the CLOUD as that user WITHOUT their
//        password. The on-prem compromise JUMPS to Entra ID.
//
//        FLAGSHIP WEAKNESS (what sets AP06 apart from AP05, which ends in DCSync
//        on-prem): the password of AZUREADSSOACC$ is NEVER rotated by default (unlike
//        normal machine accounts, which rotate every 30 days) ->
//        its hash, stolen once, is valid FOREVER = eternal persistence.
//
// Source: Windows SECURITY events from the hybrid DC (on-prem in Azure IaaS,
//         the SAME DC shared with AP05), ingested into Log Analytics / Sentinel
//         via Azure Monitor Agent (AMA) + Data Collection Rule. Table: SecurityEvent.
//         AP06 covers ONLY the ON-PREM leg: the effective authentication to the CLOUD with
//         the forged ticket would be seen in Entra ID (SigninLogs of Seamless SSO /
//         AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs), but the lab does NOT provision the tenant or
//         Entra Connect -> that leg is DECLARED not covered (just as AP05 declares
//         there are no AuditLogs/SigninLogs). See "CLOUD LIMIT" at the end.
//
// MITRE:  T1552     (Unsecured Credentials: the Entra Connect sync credential with DCSync)
//         T1003.006 (OS Credential Dumping: DCSync — extracts the keys of AZUREADSSOACC$) [CORE]
//         T1558.001 (Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Silver Ticket over AZUREADSSOACC$)
//         T1550     (Use Alternate Authentication Material: the forged ticket authenticates to the CLOUD)
//
// EVENTS (Windows Security):
//   4662  An operation was performed on a Directory Service object (DCSync =
//         Access with the replication property GUIDs DS-Replication-Get-Changes)
//   4769  A Kerberos service ticket (TGS) was requested — here for the SPN of
//         Seamless SSO (silver ticket hunt, WITH caveat; see SIGNAL B)
//   4741  A computer account was created (the seeding of AZUREADSSOACC$)
//   4742  A computer account was changed (SPNs / password of AZUREADSSOACC$)
//
// AUDIT PREREQUISITE (enabled by seed-ap06, gated on enable_ap06, NOT the shared DSC
// — so AP05 stays intact; it is idempotent and persists across seed/-Remediate):
//   auditpol /set /subcategory:"Directory Service Access"            /success:enable /failure:enable  (4662)
//   auditpol /set /subcategory:"Kerberos Service Ticket Operations"  /success:enable                   (4769)
//   auditpol /set /subcategory:"Computer Account Management"         /success:enable                   (4741/4742)
//   4662 ALSO requires an audit SACL on the domain naming context;
//   WITHOUT audit + SACL the event is NOT generated and the detection fires empty.
//
// CAVEATS OF THE REAL SecurityEvent SCHEMA (verified against the Azure Monitor
// table reference):
//   * The 4662 property GUIDs (DS-Replication-Get-Changes) are NOT a dedicated
//     column: they travel inside 'Properties' (and, as a fallback, in the raw XML of
//     'EventData'). They are searched in both locations with OR (never an AND that discards
//     a branch — lesson from AP04/AP05/AP07).
//   * 4662 is HIGH VOLUME and the REAL Azure AD Connect sync account generates
//     constant legitimate DCSync -> in production the known sync account is
//     allowlisted. In the lab, the DCSync by the seeded account IS the signal.
//   * 4769: SecurityEvent exposes ServiceName / TicketEncryptionType as columns;
//     if your pipeline does not promote them, they are extracted from the XML of 'EventData'. RC4 = 0x17.
//   * The target SID of the DCSync (naming context / AZUREADSSOACC$) is NOT always
//     promoted: the strong signature is replication GUID + SubjectUserName = the sync account.
//
// SUPPLEMENT: Microsoft Defender for Identity brings native detection of "Suspected
//             DCSync attack" and "Suspected Golden/Silver Ticket". Use in parallel.
//             If a real tenant exists, supplement with a KQL over SigninLogs
//             of Seamless SSO (autologon.microsoftazuread-sso.com) — see CLOUD LIMIT.
//
// Author: Azure Attack Range   Date: 2026-11-15
// =====================================================================

let sso_account   = "AZUREADSSOACC";      // = var.sso_account_name  (output ap06_sso_account without the '$')
let sync_account  = "Sync_AZRANGE";       // = var.sync_account_name (output ap06_sync_account)
let sso_spn       = "autologon.microsoftazuread-sso.com";  // SPN that Entra validates and that the detections catch
// Directory replication property GUIDs (the DCSync signature):
let repl_changes     = "1131f6aa-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2";  // DS-Replication-Get-Changes
let repl_changes_all = "1131f6ad-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2";  // DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All
let lookback      = 1d;
let corr_window   = 10m;                  // window DCSync(4662) -> TGS SSO(4769) for OUTPUT 2

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGNAL A (FLAGSHIP) — T1003.006: DCSync. 4662 (Directory Service Access) whose
//           'Properties' contains the replication GUIDs DS-Replication-Get-Changes
//           and/or -All, by the SYNCHRONIZATION account, over the naming context /
//           AZUREADSSOACC$. It is the signature of extracting the Kerberos keys of
//           AZUREADSSOACC$. In the lab, the seeded sync account doing DCSync IS
//           the signal; in prod the legitimate Azure AD Connect account is allowlisted.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
let SignalA_dcsync =
    SecurityEvent
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where EventID == 4662
    | where Properties has repl_changes or Properties has repl_changes_all
         or EventData has repl_changes or EventData has repl_changes_all
    | project TimeGenerated,
              Signal   = "A-dcsync-4662",
              Stage    = "T1003.006 — DCSync (replication GUID) = extraction of the keys of AZUREADSSOACC$",
              EventID,
              Actor    = SubjectUserName,
              Host     = Computer,
              ObjectName,
              ObjectServer,
              Properties,
              AccessMask;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGNAL B (silver ticket HUNT, LOAD-BEARING CAVEAT) — 4769: Kerberos TGS for the
//           Seamless SSO SPN (HTTP/autologon.microsoftazuread-sso.com or
//           AZUREADSSOACC$), with interest in RC4 encryption (0x17).
//   *** CRITICAL CAVEAT ***: a silver ticket FORGED OFFLINE with the stolen key
//           does NOT pass through the KDC -> it often does NOT generate a 4769 on the DC.
//           That is why this signal is a HUNT / baseline (it captures legitimate Seamless
//           SSO traffic and TGS anomalies), NEVER an AND gate of the chain: requiring 4769
//           would silently discard the forge itself (the same mistake we avoid in AP07).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
let SignalB_sso_tgs =
    SecurityEvent
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where EventID == 4769
    | extend SvcName = coalesce(column_ifexists("ServiceName", dynamic(null)),
                                extract(@'Name="ServiceName">\s*([^<]*?)\s*<', 1, EventData))
    | extend EncType = coalesce(column_ifexists("TicketEncryptionType", dynamic(null)),
                                extract(@'Name="TicketEncryptionType">\s*([^<]*?)\s*<', 1, EventData))
    | where SvcName has sso_account or SvcName contains sso_spn
    | project TimeGenerated,
              Signal   = "B-sso-tgs-4769",
              Stage    = "T1558.001 — TGS for the Seamless SSO SPN (hunt; the offline forge may NOT generate 4769)",
              EventID,
              Actor    = TargetUserName,
              Host     = Computer,
              SourceIp = IpAddress,
              ServiceName = SvcName,
              TicketEncryptionType = EncType;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGNAL C (seeding visibility) — 4741 (computer account created) / 4742 (computer
//           account modified) over AZUREADSSOACC$. The very seeding by
//           seed-ap06 (creating AZUREADSSOACC$ with the SPNs) and the -Remediate (rotating its
//           key 2x) fire here: the defender SEES the vuln being planted / rotated
//           (analogous to 4899/4900 in AP05). 'TargetUserName' = AZUREADSSOACC$.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
let SignalC_ssoacct_change =
    SecurityEvent
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where EventID in (4741, 4742)
    | where TargetUserName has sso_account
    | project TimeGenerated,
              Signal   = "C-ssoacct-change",
              Stage    = iff(EventID == 4741,
                             "seeding — AZUREADSSOACC$ CREATED (4741)",
                             "seeding/rotation — AZUREADSSOACC$ MODIFIED (4742)"),
              EventID,
              Actor    = SubjectUserName,
              Host     = Computer,
              TargetUserName,
              TargetDomainName;

// =====================================================================
// OUTPUT 1 — Unified timeline of the 3 AP06 signals (INDEPENDENT).
// Triage of the full chain (exploit):
//   SIGNAL C (seeding of AZUREADSSOACC$)  ->  SIGNAL A (4662: DCSync by the sync
//   account = extraction of the keys of AZUREADSSOACC$)  ->  SIGNAL B (4769 optional:
//   TGS of the SSO SPN; the silver ticket may NOT touch the DC). Each signal fires ON
//   ITS OWN (union, NEVER a global AND that discards a branch when an event is missing).
// =====================================================================
union isfuzzy=true SignalA_dcsync, SignalB_sso_tgs, SignalC_ssoacct_change
| sort by TimeGenerated asc


// =====================================================================
// OUTPUT 2 (high fidelity) — Correlate DCSync -> TGS of the SSO SPN.
// 4662 DCSync (replication GUID) by the sync account  ==>  within
// corr_window and on the same host, a 4769 for the Seamless SSO SPN. NOTE: the
// CAVEAT of SIGNAL B still holds — a silver ticket FORGED OFFLINE may NOT
// generate the 4769, so this correlation is a fidelity BOOSTER for the case
// where the attacker DOES request the TGS, NOT a requirement of the chain. That is why it is left
// COMMENTED and as a complement to OUTPUT 1 (union), not in its place. Uncomment
// to alert on the scenario "DCSync followed by use of the SSO SPN".
// =====================================================================
// let dcsync_ssoacc =
//     SecurityEvent
//     | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
//     | where EventID == 4662
//     | where Properties has repl_changes or Properties has repl_changes_all
//          or EventData has repl_changes or EventData has repl_changes_all
//     | project DcsyncTime = TimeGenerated, Host = Computer, SyncActor = SubjectUserName;
// let sso_tgs =
//     SecurityEvent
//     | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
//     | where EventID == 4769
//     | extend SvcName = coalesce(column_ifexists("ServiceName", dynamic(null)),
//                                 extract(@'Name="ServiceName">\s*([^<]*?)\s*<', 1, EventData))
//     | where SvcName has "AZUREADSSOACC" or SvcName has "autologon.microsoftazuread-sso.com"
//     | project TgsTime = TimeGenerated, Host = Computer, TgsUser = TargetUserName,
//               TgsIp = IpAddress, ServiceName = SvcName;
// dcsync_ssoacc
// | join kind=inner sso_tgs on Host
// | where TgsTime between (DcsyncTime .. (DcsyncTime + corr_window))
// | project DcsyncTime, SyncActor, TgsTime, TgsUser, TgsIp, ServiceName,
//           Alert = "AP06: DCSync by the sync account -> TGS of the Seamless SSO SPN (possible use of the silver ticket)"
// | sort by DcsyncTime asc


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPERTY HUNT (NOT event-driven; declared as a complement) — the tell of
// non-rotation: AZUREADSSOACC$ with an OLD / frozen PasswordLastSet. Normal machine
// accounts rotate their password every ~30 days; AZUREADSSOACC$ has no host
// to rotate it -> an old PasswordLastSet is precisely the persistence primitive.
// This is NOT in SecurityEvent: it requires a periodic LDAP query, e.g. from
// the DC itself:
//   Get-ADComputer -Filter "sAMAccountName -eq 'AZUREADSSOACC$'" -Properties PasswordLastSet |
//     Select-Object Name, PasswordLastSet
//   # Alert if (Get-Date) - PasswordLastSet > 30 days.
// In Sentinel, if you inventory AD objects into a custom table, compare PasswordLastSet
// against the threshold there; without that inventory, it is a manual/scheduled hunt.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// CLOUD LIMIT (DECLARED, NOT covered by the lab) — the leg that CLOSES the chain:
// the EFFECTIVE authentication to the cloud with the forged ticket (T1550), impersonating
// a synchronized user via Seamless SSO, would be seen in Entra ID:
//   SigninLogs | where ResourceDisplayName has "autologon.microsoftazuread-sso.com"
//              // or AppDisplayName / AuthenticationDetails of the Seamless SSO flow
//   AADServicePrincipalSignInLogs  // for the Seamless SSO SPN
// The lab does NOT provision the tenant or Entra Connect, so these tables do NOT exist
// here: AP06 covers ONLY the ON-PREM leg (just as AP05 declares there are no
// on-prem AuditLogs/SigninLogs). To close end-to-end detection in a real
// environment: (1) Microsoft Defender for Identity (Suspected DCSync / Suspected
// Golden/Silver Ticket, native over the DC traffic), and (2) a companion KQL
// over SigninLogs of Seamless SSO when a connected tenant exists.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
